---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PdfSecureToMarkdown from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/PdfSecureToMarkdown.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="pdf-to-markdown">
  <PdfSecureToMarkdown client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <section>
      <h2>A PDF does not contain paragraphs</h2>
      <p>
        A PDF page is a list of drawing instructions: put this glyph at this coordinate in this font
        at this size, then the next one. The file has no paragraphs, headings, lists or tables, only
        positions. So a PDF to Markdown tool must rebuild structure that was never recorded, and the
        question is how well it does that.
      </p>
      <p>Here is what this one infers, and from what:</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Markdown element</th>
              <th scope="col">Inferred from</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Lines</td>
              <td>Glyph runs clustered by baseline, within 40% of the local font size</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Word spaces</td>
              <td>Horizontal gaps wider than about 0.28 of the font size</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Paragraph breaks</td>
              <td>A vertical gap more than 1.75× the line height</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Headings</td>
              <td>
                Font sizes more than 12% above the size that carries the most characters, ranked
                descending into levels 1–4
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Bold</td>
              <td>The embedded font's own bold flag, or "Bold/Black/Semibold" in its name</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Bullets</td>
              <td>A leading •, ‣, ▪, –, - or * followed by a space</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Numbered lists</td>
              <td>A leading <code>1.</code>, <code>a)</code> or <code>iv.</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Code</td>
              <td>A line set entirely in a monospaced font</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Tables</td>
              <td>Three or more consecutive lines that split into the same number of columns</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Why Markdown is the right format for feeding an LLM</h2>
      <p>
        Language models read structure. Given a wall of undifferentiated text they will find the
        answer eventually; given headings, bullets and tables they follow the document's own
        hierarchy, which measurably improves retrieval and summarisation. Markdown is also the
        markup they saw most during training, and it is compact: no closing tags use up your context
        window.
      </p>
      <p>
        The character count under the result includes a rough token estimate at four characters per
        token, which is close enough to tell you whether a document will fit in a given context
        window before you paste it.
      </p>
      <p>
        One thing to be clear about: the <strong>conversion</strong> happens here and goes nowhere.
        The <strong>paste</strong> afterwards goes to whoever runs the chatbot. If the document is
        one you would not upload to a converter, think about that second step too.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Running heads, page numbers and other noise</h2>
      <p>
        Printed documents repeat things that make no sense in a Markdown file: the document title at
        the top of every page, a page number at the bottom, a confidentiality notice in the footer.
        With "strip running heads" enabled, the tool normalises the top two and bottom two lines of
        every page (replacing digits with a placeholder so "Page 4" and "Page 5" count as the same
        line), and drops anything that appears on 60 per cent or more of the pages.
      </p>
      <p>
        Turn it off if your document is short, or if an important line repeats, for example a
        recurring section label in a form.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What will not convert well</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Scans: with no text layer, there is nothing to read. The tool tells you so and does not
          give an empty file. Reading pixels needs OCR, which this tool does not include, because
          OCR engines small enough for a browser are not accurate enough.
        </li>
        <li>
          Multi-column layouts. Academic papers and newspapers interleave columns
          in reading order that geometry alone cannot always recover. Expect lines from two columns
          to alternate.
        </li>
        <li>
          Complex tables. Merged cells, nested headers and ragged alignment defeat
          the column detector. Turn table detection off if it is producing nonsense.
        </li>
        <li>
          Mathematics. Equations are laid out as individually positioned glyphs,
          often in a symbol font. They come out as scrambled characters, not LaTeX.
        </li>
        <li>
          Ligatures and hyphenation. Some PDFs encode "fi" as one glyph with no
          Unicode mapping, and hyphenated line breaks are rejoined only when the previous line ends
          in a hyphen.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Where to go instead</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Want the words with no structure at all? <a href="/pdf-to-text">PDF to text</a> is faster
          and has fewer ways to be wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          Want an editable document? <a href="/pdf-to-word">PDF to Word</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Want the tables as data? <a href="/pdf-to-excel">PDF to Excel</a> or
          <a href="/pdf-to-csv">PDF to CSV</a> use the same column clustering with a control for the
          snapping tolerance.
        </li>
        <li>
          Going the other way? <a href="/markdown-to-pdf">Markdown to PDF</a> and
          <a href="/markdown-to-word">Markdown to Word</a>.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
